        "title": "Salary Negotiation — Pehli Job Ka Sabse Bada Decision (Part 3)",
        "content": "I'm committed to making this work and joining [Company].\n\nWarm regards,\n[Your Name]\n```\n\n### What to Negotiate Beyond Salary\n\n| Benefit | Typical Value | Negotiable? | How to Ask |\n|---------|--------------|-------------|------------|\n| **Joining Bonus** | ₹20,000-1,00,000 | Yes | \"Could there be a joining bonus?\" |\n| **Relocation Allowance** | ₹10,000-50,000 | Yes | \"I'm relocating. Is relocation support available?\" |\n| **Work From Home** | ₹2,000-5,000/month saved | Yes | \"Would 2-3 days WFH be possible?\" |\n| **Learning Budget** | ₹10,000-50,000/year | Yes | \"Is there a learning/training budget?\" |\n| **Flexible Hours** | Quality of life | Yes | \"Could I have flexible timing?\" |\n| **Extra Leave** | 2-5 days/year | Yes | \"Could I get additional leave days?\" |\n| **Performance Bonus** | 5-15% of CTC | Yes | \"Could we structure a performance bonus?\" |\n| **Stock Options (ESOP)** | Variable | Yes (startups) | \"What's the ESOP policy?\" |\n| **Health Insurance** | ₹5-10 Lakh cover | Yes | \"Is family health insurance included?\" |\n\n### Common Mistakes in Salary Negotiation\n\n| Mistake | Why It's Bad | What to Do Instead |\n|---------|-------------|-------------------|\n| \"Jo bhi offer hai, theek hai\" | Free money chhod rahe ho | Always ask — worst case mein \"no\" bolenge |\n| Not researching market rate | Blindly negotiate kar rahe ho | Research karo pehle |\n| Accepting immediately | Desperate lagta hai | \"I'd like 24-48 hours to review\" bolo |\n| Making it personal | \"Mujhe zyada chahiye\" vs data | Data-based arguments do |\n| Not having BATNA | Negotiate karna mushkil | Multiple offers rakho |\n| Not getting offer in writing | Verbal promise != commitment | Written offer letter lo |\n| Negotiating over email when call is better | Tone lost hota hai | Phone/video call pe negotiate karo |\n| Revealing your number first | Tumhari expectation low ho sakti hai | Let them make the first offer |\n| Making it emotional | \"I need more money\" vs \"Market rate\" | Use data, not emotions |\n| Not negotiating beyond salary | Sirf CTC dekha, benefits bhool gaye | WFH, learning budget, joining bonus |\n| Forgetting taxes | ₹5 LPA vs ₹6 LPA — tax difference | Net take-home calculate karo |\n| Accepting verbal commitment | \"HR ne bola tha\" = meaningless | Written offer letter lo |\n\n### Salary Negotiation Psychology — What's Really Happening\n\n**Why Companies Expect Negotiation:**\n- 70% of managers budget for negotiation\n- They expect you to counter — it's part of the process\n- Not negotiating signals lack of confidence\n- Companies respect candidates who know their worth\n\n**Why Companies Say \"No\":**\n- Budget constraints (genuine)\n- Standardized pay bands (genuine)\n- Testing your persistence (strategic)\n- They think you'll accept anyway (arrogant)\n\n**What Happens After You Negotiate:**\n- Company respects you more (usually)\n- Your starting point is higher for future raises\n- You've established yourself as someone who knows their value\n- No negative impact on your job (if done professionally)\n\n### Salary Negotiation for Different Industries\n\n| Industry | Typical Fresher CTC | Negotiation Room | Best Strategy |\n|----------|--------------------|-----------------|--------------|\n| **IT Services (TCS, Infosys, Wipro)** | ₹3.5-4.5 LPA | Low (10-15%) | Joining bonus, WFH |\n| **Product Companies (Google, Microsoft)** | ₹12-25 LPA | Medium (15-25%) | Competitive offers |\n| **Startups** | ₹4-8 LPA | High (20-40%) | Equity + cash negotiation |\n| **Consulting (McKinsey, BCG)** | ₹15-25 LPA | Low-Medium | Signing bonus |\n| **Banking (Goldman Sachs, JP Morgan)** | ₹8-15 LPA | Medium | Performance bonus |\n| **Core Engineering (L&T, Tata Steel)** | ₹4-6 LPA | Low (10-15%) | Benefits, training |\n| **Government Jobs** | Fixed pay scale | None | — |\n| **FMCG (HUL, P&G)** | ₹6-10 LPA | Medium | Brand manager fast-track |\n\n### Real Example: Sneha Negotiated ₹3 LPA Extra on Her First Job\n\n**Background:** Sneha, final year CS student from tier-2 college. Placement season mein 3 offers mile:\n\n1. **Wipro:** ₹3.6 LPA\n2. **TCS:** ₹4.0 LPA\n3. **Mid-size Startup:** ₹5.5 LPA\n\nSneha ka dream company tha **Freshworks**. Usne apply kiya through referral. Offer aaya — ₹5.0 LPA.\n\n**Sneha's Research:**\n- Glassdoor: Freshworks fresher salary ₹4.5-6.5 LPA\n- LinkedIn: Freshworks SDE-1 ₹5-7 LPA\n- Senior se baat: \"₹5.5 LPA is the standard for good candidates\"\n\n**Sneha's Negotiation:**\n\n**Sneha:** \"Thank you for the offer! I'm very excited about Freshworks. After doing some research on market rates and considering my internship experience where I built a full-stack app that handled 10,000+ users, I was hoping we could discuss the compensation. The range I've seen is ₹5.5-6.5 LPA. Would ₹6 LPA be possible?\"\n\n**HR:** \"The offer is competitive for a fresher. We can't go beyond ₹5 LPA for this band.\"\n\n**Sneha:** \"I understand. Could we perhaps look at a signing bonus? I also have a competing offer at ₹5.5 LPA. Freshworks is my first choice, but the ₹1 LPA difference is significant for me.\"\n\n**HR:** \"Let me check... We can offer ₹5.2 LPA with a ₹25,000 signing bonus.\"\n\n**Sneha:** \"Could we do ₹5.5 LPA with the signing bonus? I'm willing to sign a 2-year commitment.\"\n\n**Result:** Sneha got ₹5.2 LPA + ₹25,000 signing bonus + guaranteed 12-month review. Over 3 years, the total impact was ₹3 LPA more than what she would have accepted at ₹4 LPA.\n\n---",
